    "d4"
   ],
   "spoken": "Against the Philidor, apply basic principles — the modest pawn to d6 is passive, closing in the bishop and developing nothing. The logical response is to open the centre with the pawn to d4, punishing the passivity with lines.",
   "kind": "plan"
  },
  {
   "ply": 6,
   "t": 48.5,
   "fen": "r1bqkbnr/pppn1ppp/3p4/4p3/3PP3/5N2/PPP2PPP/RNBQKB1R w KQkq - 1 4",
   "line": [
    "Nd7"
   ],
   "spoken": ""
  },
  {
   "ply": 7,
   "t": 89.5,
   "fen": "r1bqkbnr/pppn1ppp/3p4/4p3/2BPP3/5N2/PPP2PPP/RNBQK2R b KQkq - 2 4",
   "line": [
    "Bc4"
   ],
   "spoken": "Passive development leaves the defender short of resources against a quick onslaught — and in the opening, always associate an onslaught with the f7 square, the only accessible weak point. So the bishop swings to c4, aiming straight at it for a fast kill.",
   "kind": "plan"
  },
  {
   "ply": 8,
   "t": 93,
   "fen": "r1bqkb1r/pppn1ppp/3p1n2/4p3/2BPP3/5N2/PPP2PPP/RNBQK2R w KQkq - 3 5",
   "line": [
    "Ngf6"
   ],
   "spoken": ""
  },
  {
   "ply": 9,
   "t": 101.5,
   "fen": "r1bqkb1r/pppn1ppp/3p1n2/4p1N1/2BPP3/8/PPP2PPP/RNBQK2R b KQkq - 4 5",
   "line": [
    "Ng5"
   ],
   "spoken": "Developing the knight to f6 is a mistake — the knight jump to g5 leaves the defence in real trouble, and a central break wins a pawn outright. It's the Fried Liver pattern, but an even worse version for the defender because they're so passive.",
   "kind": "tactic"
  },
